        "paragraphs": [
          "To casual observers, a crown wildfire sweeping through a dry woodland appears wholly destructive, reducing living canopies to blackened skeletons. Yet across fire-prone landscapes of Australia, California, and the Mediterranean basin, periodic flames have acted for millions of years as a potent evolutionary sculptor. Rather than merely enduring fire, numerous woody species—most famously Australian eucalyptus trees and certain North American pines—have evolved specialized anatomical and reproductive traits that actually depend on intense heat to complete their life cycles.",
          "Eucalyptus species display an astonishing dual strategy for surviving and even courting fire. Their shedding strips of dry, ribbon-like bark and leaves rich in volatile cineole oils readily carry flames upward, turning minor brushfires into fierce conflagrations that eliminate competing shade-tolerant plants. Meanwhile, the eucalyptus trees themselves withstand the inferno through epicormic buds tucked deep beneath thick, insulating bark, as well as subterranean woody swellings called lignotubers. Within weeks of a blaze, these protected tissues sprout vigorous new foliage, giving the trees an immediate head start in the sunlit, ash-fertilized soil.",
          "Equally remarkable is serotiny, a reproductive adaptation seen in lodgepole pines, giant sequoias, and several banksia shrubs. Serotinous species retain mature seeds inside hard, resin-sealed cones that can remain closed on the branches for decades. Only when the searing heat of a passing wildfire melts the resinous seal do the scales flex open, showering thousands of seeds onto the freshly cleared forest floor. By timing germination to follow a fire, seedlings escape dense canopy shade and encounter fewer seed-eating rodents and fungal pathogens.",
          "However, ecologists warn that anthropogenic climate change is now testing the limits of these ancient adaptations. While fire-adapted flora evolved under predictable intervals of fifteen to fifty years, hotter droughts are triggering mega-fires at much shorter frequencies. If a second severe blaze strikes before young saplings mature enough to produce new seed banks or lignotubers, even the most resilient pyrophyte ecosystems can collapse into impoverished grasslands."
        ]
